TUTORIALS
To master the role of a Deep Learning Framework and Compiler Engineer, you must bridge the gap between high-level AI research and low-level system performance.
1. Essential Framework Foundations
Before diving into compilers, you must understand how frameworks represent and execute models.
PyTorch Internals: Learn to migrate Python models to TorchScript for production and understand its C++ frontend to see how the engine handles tensors.
TensorFlow XLA: Explore Accelerated Linear Algebra (XLA), which is TensorFlow's domain-specific compiler for optimizing model graphs.
Hands-on Guide: The book PyTorch Deep Learning Hands-On covers the full workflow from prototyping to high-performance deployment.
2. Deep Learning Compiler Toolchains
The industry relies on specific open-source stacks to automate hardware-specific optimizations. 
Apache TVM: This is the industry-standard end-to-end compiler stack. Follow the TVM Getting Started Guide to learn how to ingest models from PyTorch/ONNX and generate optimized kernels for CPUs, GPUs, or FPGAs.
MLIR (Multi-Level Intermediate Representation): Developed by the LLVM Project, MLIR is a "meta-compiler" used to build other compilers. Use the Toy Tutorial to learn how to define language-specific dialects and perform high-level graph rewrites.
LLVM: Since most ML compilers (like TVM and XLA) use LLVM as their backend, mastering LLVM IR (Intermediate Representation) is critical for low-level code generation. 
3. Performance & Hardware Optimization
Translating models is only half the battle; they must be fast.
GPU Programming: Take the Johns Hopkins GPU Programming Specialization to master CUDA and C++ for parallel execution.
Optimization Techniques: Study Quantization (reducing precision to INT8), Pruning (removing redundant weights), and Operator Fusion (combining multiple layers into one kernel).
Targeting the Edge: For mobile/IoT, refer to Deep Learning with MATLAB and Python for tutorials on deploying to NVIDIA Jetson and ARM Cortex-M. 
